									</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dr-hajovsky" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7041744); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7041744&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607912718" alt="Dr Hajovsky" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7041744)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dr-hajovsky" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7041744); return false;" >Dr Hajovsky</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607912107 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">14 Dec 2020 02:15</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13930238/love-at-long-last">Love at Long Last</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4844200">
									

<p>(Can't give greenlights)</p>
<p>To start off, I really <em>really</em> like this narrative. Dear Nia is a really great story with themes and technology that would be right at home in the foundation universe. Of course, as has already been noted, the main focus in writing will need to be on changing certain aspects of the story so that it can't be considered plagiarism.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Show&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Begone&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>&#8230;the primary one being that Chase's universe orbits a Red Dwarf while Underwood's earth (our Earth) orbits a Yellow dwarf.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>While this does present some really cool imagery, it might need some minor explanation in the article, as the earth would be a very different place if it were orbiting a red dwarf. Orbiting a different class of star but still resulting in nearly identical timelines is a bit of a stretch beyond the multiverse theory. If you wanted to keep Chase's sun a Red Dwarf, perhaps you could explain by saying the mass of the sun was changed due to advance foundation testing or anomalies in that dimension? Ultimately though, this is only likely to be noticed by astro and quantum physics nerds like me and won't detract from the narrative.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This leads to an event known as &quot;Gothic Storm&quot;, the first known Reality-Merging Class Scenario (RMK-Class) in that reality. The aftermath is chaotic, and leads to public awareness of the Foundation, the release of numerous anomalies, and shifts of entire galaxies in the universe.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I really like these consequences, as it does something the original story doesn't: showing the direct consequences of not letting go. However, since reality ending scenarios don't carry much weight in a narrative like this, perhaps also having a more personal consequence could give the narrative more of a punch. Perhaps either Underwood or Chase has a child, but due to Underwood's persistence in contacting Chase, the collapsing realities causes the death of the child. This gives us a more emotional and relatable consequence to Underwood not being able to let go and could show her that her actions have only caused more suffering to those she has left. This would also help set this story apart from Dear Nia.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dr. Underwood had seemingly disappeared, while Dr. Chase was discovered in her office. The document ends with an interview with Chase, and remains the only documentation of this event.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>While this does give the narrative some mystery and a feeling of being unresolved, it feels a bit out of place. IMO, Underwood should not disappear, or at least be allowed to at least give a final log or message that shows us whether she's realized the weight and consequences of her actions or not. This could help wrap up the story's theme and transition better into the interview with Chase.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>With a few minor tweaks, I would definitely give this a greenlight if I could.</p>

							</div>

								
					
